#: src/reference/reference/plugin.md:11
msgid "Plugin"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/plugin.md:14
msgid ""
"```admonish note\n"
"This page covers plugin details for intermediate users.\n"
"See [Plugin basics][plugin-basics] for the general introduction of the "
"plugin system.\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/plugin.md:19
msgid "Description"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/plugin.md:21
msgid ""
"A _plugin_ is a way to extend the build definition, often to add "
"capabilities beyond the built-in settings and tasks. Some of the example "
"usages includes building Docker containers, supporting to Scala.JS, and "
"generate GitHub Actions YAML."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/plugin.md:23
msgid ""
"A plugin defines a sequence of sbt settings that are automatically added to "
"all projects or that are explicitly declared for selected projects. A plugin "
"can also define new commands. The plugins in sbt 1.x and 2.x extend the "
"`sbt.AutoPlugin` trait, which automates the setting orders between dependent "
"plugins."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/plugin.md:29
msgid "Creating a plugin"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/plugin.md:31
msgid ""
"Conceptually we can think of the sbt plugins to be _any other libraries on "
"the metabuild_, which is currently on Scala 3.8.2. However, in practice "
"modern sbt plugins are built around auto plugin (`sbt.AutoPlugin`), which "
"provides tasks and settings in the correct order. See the Plugin "
"dependencies section for more details."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/plugin.md:33
msgid "build.sbt setup"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/plugin.md:35
msgid "To make an auto plugin, create a project and enable `SbtPlugin`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/plugin.md:37
msgid ""
"````admonish example title=\"build.sbt\"\n"
"```scala\n"
"version := \"0.1.0-SNAPSHOT\"\n"
"organization := \"com.example\"\n"
"homepage := Some(url(\"https://github.com/sbt/sbt-hello\"))\n"
"\n"
"lazy val root = rootProject\n"
"  .enablePlugins(SbtPlugin)\n"
"  .settings(\n"
"    name := \"sbt-hello\",\n"
"    pluginCrossBuild / sbtVersion := {\n"
"      scalaBinaryVersion.value match\n"
"        case \"3\"    => \"2.0.0-RC10\" // set minimum sbt version\n"
"    }\n"
"  )\n"
"```\n"
"````"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/plugin.md:55
msgid ""
"```admonish note\n"
"- sbt 2.x plugins must be compiled with Scala 3.x. By not specifying "
"`scalaVersion`, sbt will default to the Scala version suited for a plugin.\n"
"- `pluginCrossBuild / sbtVersion` is a setting to compile your plugin "
"against an _older_ version of sbt, which allows the plugin users to choose "
"from a range of sbt versions.\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/plugin.md:60
msgid "projectSettings"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/plugin.md:62
msgid ""
"In an appropriate namespace, define your auto plugin object by extending "
"`sbt.AutoPlugin`. With auto plugins, all provided settings are provided by "
"the plugin directly via the `projectSettings` method. Here's an example "
"plugin that adds a task named `hello` to the subprojects:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/plugin.md:65
msgid ""
"````admonish example title=\"src/main/sbthello/HelloPlugin.scala\"\n"
"```scala\n"
"package sbthello\n"
"\n"
"import sbt.*\n"
"import Keys.*\n"
"\n"
"object HelloPlugin extends AutoPlugin:\n"
"  override def trigger = allRequirements\n"
"\n"
"  object autoImport:\n"
"    val helloGreeting = settingKey[String](\"greeting\")\n"
"    val hello = taskKey[Unit](\"say hello\")\n"
"  end autoImport\n"
"\n"
"  import autoImport.*\n"
"  override lazy val globalSettings: Seq[Setting[?]] = Seq(\n"
"    helloGreeting := \"hi\",\n"
"  )\n"
"\n"
"  override lazy val projectSettings: Seq[Setting[?]] = Seq(\n"
"    hello := {\n"
"      val s = streams.value\n"
"      val g = helloGreeting.value\n"
"      s.log.info(g)\n"
"    }\n"
"  )\n"
"end HelloPlugin\n"
"```\n"
"````"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/plugin.md:96
msgid ""
"```admonish note title=\"def vs lazy val\"\n"
"In Scala, a `def` method can be overridden using a `lazy val`, using a "
"mechanism called _universal access principle_.\n"
"We recommending using `lazy val` for `projectSettings` since the setting "
"definitions tend to be immutable.\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/plugin.md:112
msgid "globalSettings"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/plugin.md:114
msgid ""
"The `globalSettings` is appended once to the global settings (for example, "
"`Global / helloGreeting`). These allow a plugin to provide new functionality "
"or new defaults. One main use of this feature is to globally add commands, "
"such as for IDE plugins."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/plugin.md:122
msgid ""
"Where possible, your plugin should define the default value using "
"`globalSettings`. Providing the value in the widest scope and using the "
"value in narrowest scope give the user the maximum flexibility to change the "
"settings."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/plugin.md:124
msgid "Implementing plugin dependencies"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/plugin.md:126
msgid "Next step is to define the plugin dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/plugin.md:139
msgid ""
"The `requires` method returns a value of type `Plugins`, which is a DSL for "
"constructing the dependency list. The requires method typically contains one "
"of the following values:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/plugin.md:141
msgid "`empty` (No plugins)"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/plugin.md:142
